Great progress! The SDIO communication is now working - CMD5 is succeeding (getting responses from the ESP32-C6). But the issue is that the CMD5 loop runs1000 times (SDIO_INIT_TIMEOUT_MS) without the card ready bit (bit 31) being set in the response.

The problem is in the CMD5 response handling. Looking at the code:

```c
ret = sdio_send_cmd(SDIO_CMD_SEND_OP_COND, 0, &resp, SDIO_RESP_R4);
if (ret < 0)
 {
      up_mdelay(1);
      continue;
    }

up_puts("SDIO: CMD5 ok\n");

/* Check if card is ready (bit 31 of R4) */
if (resp & (1 << 31))
    {
      SDIO_INFO("SDIO card ready, OCR=0x%08lx\n", (unsigned long)resp);
      break;
    }

/* Send CMD5 with voltage window to power up card */
ret = sdio_send_cmd(SDIO_CMD_SEND_OP_COND, 0x00FF8000,
                    &resp, SDIO_RESP_R4);
up_mdelay(10);
```

The issue is that the first CMD5 query returns a response, but bit 31 isn't set yet. Then we send a second CMD5 with the voltage window to actually power up the card, but we're not checking if that response has the ready bit set before looping again.

Looking at the response format for R4, I need to verify what the actual resp value is to understand why the card isn't transitioning to ready state. The issue is that bit 31 of the R4 response should indicate the card is ready, but it's never getting set even though CMD5 is succeeding. I'm wondering if the response isn't being read correctly from the register, or if there's an issue with how the SDIO initialization sequence is being handled.

Actually, I think I see the problem now. The code sends CMD5 with arg=0 first to query the voltage window, then sends it again with the actual voltage window (0x00FF8000). But on the second iteration, it's querying with arg=0 again instead of continuing to send the voltage window, which would reset the card's state and prevent it from ever becoming ready.

The fix is to keep sending CMD5 with the voltage window argument until bit 31 in the response indicates the card is ready, rather than alternating between querying and sending the voltage window.

